rimary abstract reports TEAD1 binding and dependence of both Pmp22 and Egr2 on TEAD1/YAP/TAZ. My coverage remains abstract plus author-thesis discussion, not a newly completed full-paper methods audit.\n+- Pantera thesis Chapter 1, printed p14 (PDF p22; sources/primary/Pantera-thesis.txt:412-424), explicitly describes TEAD-site mutation in luciferase constructs containing B, C or intronic enhancer sequences, separately from rat primary/S16 Tead1 knockdown and in-vivo YAP/TAZ loss. These mutations are reporters, not edited endogenous TEAD sites. The thesis account is contextual author synthesis of Lopez-Anido et al. 2016, not a replacement for its final methods.\n+- Thesis Chapter 2, printed pp62-63 (PDF pp70-71; text lines1695-1731), links TEAD1 occupancy at B/C and reported P1 bias to whole-super-enhancer deletion. The rat S16-derived clone experiment measures steady-state first-exon RNA, not TEAD-motif-specific necessity under extrinsic perturbation or calibrated initiation.\n+- PMC7145652 Figure1D/Methods is a nearby human NRG1/dbcAMP context (seven-day differentiation and total PMP22 RT-qPCR), but its edited target is the P1 TATA box, not a TEAD site. Its separate C22 first-exon measurements cannot fill that gap.\n+\n+The existing matrix artifact_c29f4ea4bba4c049e5387ad507ab14d3a25699b80017b0896bcca3bb62114f48 and bundle artifact_2898ce0c7c1ac558f70cd34012400744153963aa374c5db9c70d1595dfd5bfa8 are reused unchanged. No new primary retrieval, raw processing, scientific reanalysis, inferred donor/timing details for PMC5181599, or new computation registration. This notebook records the substantive handoff and the final response is left to automatic posting.\n*** End Patch"}, "timestamp": 1791228233807}
